{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Honor your father.</b> Behold, He has completed everything that we are obligated in regarding the Creator Himself and His honor, and He returned to command us in matters concerning created beings, and He began with the father, for he is to his offspring like the manner of a creator who partners in formation — for HaShem is our first father, and the one who begets is our last father. Therefore it says in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 5:16), 'just as I commanded you in My honor, so I command you in the honor of the one who partners with Me in your formation.' And the verse did not specify the honor, for it is inferred from the honor stated above regarding the first Father, blessed be He: that one acknowledges him as his father and does not deny him by saying of another man that he is his father, and does not treat him as a son for inheritance purposes, or for another matter that he expects of him, and does not bear his father's name and swear by the life of his father for naught and for falsehood. And other things enter into the category of honor, for we are commanded in all His honor, and they are spelled out in the words of our Sages (Kiddushin 31b), and they have already said (ibid. 30b) that his honor is likened to the honor of the Place [i.e., HaShem]. And just as this commandment is among the lower commandments, so He gave its reward as length of days in the land that He will give us. And according to the view of our Sages (Kiddushin 39b), the meaning of the verse <b>'that your days may be long upon the earth'</b> is that He promises that through this commandment all our days will be long, for HaShem will fill our days in this world, and they will be long in the World to Come, which is entirely long, and our dwelling shall be forever upon the good land that He will give us. And in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 5:16) He specified, 'that your days may be long, and that it may go well with you, upon the earth' — and behold, these are two promises:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The phrase 'in all His honor' (בכל כבודו) is textually difficult; read hyper-literally. It may refer back to divine honor as the paradigm, or may be a corruption. The word 'Place' (המקום) is a rabbinic epithet for God, here clarified parenthetically."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:11:54.162947029Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}